{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1010956-what-is-fast-casting-also-what-should-a-rote-be-mechanically","id":1010956,"title":"What is \"fast-casting\"? Also what should a rote be, mechanically?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-12-01T23:34:34-08:00","id":1010956,"title":"What is \"fast-casting\"? Also what should a rote be, mechanically?","content":"When I first saw fast-casting referenced in a table for magic modifiers in Second Edition--and no where else in Second Edition--I assumed it meant Focus-using magic using less time than the Focus usually requires. Then somewhere it entered my brain, possibly from a supplement, that fast-casting is using magic without a rote. This would be a pretty major revelation for something not even expanded on in the core rulebook. In the system characterized by freeform magic, freeform magic is a diversion from the default?<br /> <br /> At these forums someone mentioned rotes do not have a difficulty modifier in M20. This is probably for the best. But it seems like there should be <i>some</i> mechanical advantage to them. Maybe it's a Focus thing, that they take less time without penalty. Or they're more efficient, and can do complex things with fewer successes than the system would suggest they should. How do other people handle rotes?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-02T01:02:07-08:00","id":1010970,"title":"","content":"I prefer Fast-Casting be interpreted as pulling off an Effect in less time than a Focus might require. It puts more emphasis on preparation, and getting one's work done right. It helps in M20 that rules like Violence-As-Focus were clarified, allowing characters to wave the Fast-Casting penalty where logically appropriate. Usually in combat, which, let's face it, is where time pressures most often come up.<br /> <br /> Conflating Rotes with regular Effects is also something I like that M20 did. Rotes, Spells, Procedures, Adjustments, and the like should be different names for the same phenomenon. It keeps things simple.<br /> <br /> That said, I wouldn't be against giving optional rules that designate a &quot;Rote&quot; as being distinct enough to offer benefits above and beyond regular Effects. Like giving it a difficulty reduction of -1, provided it's a well researched or mastered Effect, and that its powers and Instruments be tightly defined. Making the difference like cooking a meal by feeling it out (an Effect) versus going by a recipe (a Rote). If you adhere to a specific formula, you reap the benefit of its familiarity. This would give characters great reason to take downtime to experiment or research, or to go out Rote trading with other Mages.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-02T03:26:16-08:00","id":1010991,"title":"","content":"There are, indeed, two definitions of fast-casting and in my opinion they don't exclude each other. The one that Bluecho mentioned:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I prefer Fast-Casting be interpreted as pulling off an Effect in less time than a Focus might require. </div> </div> </div>And the one that simply means &quot;Casting without a rote&quot;. Which essentially boils down to &quot;Making up a way to achieve an effect on the fly&quot;.<br /> The latter simply translates to the Mage, due to pressure and/or sudden need to react, works within their Paradigm and Belief system to quickly come up with a makeshift way of casting an effect.<br /> Essentially creating a new, unpracticed groundwork for what later can become a new Rote in their arsenal through practice and refinement.<br /> <br /> Both work, and I think both work well for the definition of Fast-Casting as a whole. Having to pull off a Rote quicker and will less refinement than the Rote usually requires, and making up something on the fly that can later become a Rote.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":". 12-02-2016, 06:53 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-02T07:46:24-08:00","id":1011075,"title":"","content":"Concerning rotes - they make the most sense to me as complex Effects that require major research to figure out, and which you can learn from a more experienced mage of your paradigm more quickly than you could figure it out on your own.<br /> <br /> Look at the Agama Re rite as an example. It's pretty complex, using three different spheres, and it allows you to travel the Underworld as a wraith. In the books, it doesn't seem that other mages with Entropy 4, Life 2, and Spirit 3 typically use/have that ability. So I'd call it a Rote; if you have the right sphere levels, you can learn it from another Euthanatos of the same paradigm and only then can you generate the Effect yourself. Otherwise, you'd need a good amount of research even after achieving the sphere requirements to replicate the Effect within your own paradigm - for example, a Son of Ether designing a Trans-Life Converter, or a Hermetic learning the right sigils to call upon Uriel, archangel of the threshold of death. I wouldn't allow a character who happens to have Entropy 4, Life 2, and Spirit 3 to randomly say without preparation, &quot;Okay, I go into the Underworld now.&quot;<br /> <br /> On the other end of the spectrum, simple effects don't really need rotes. The books list &quot;No-mind&quot; as a rote/common effect for the Akashics, whereby they empty their own mind of thoughts to better sense other minds. It's a one-dot effect for a single sphere, so I expect all characters with Mind 1 can come up with a way to do this on the fly. &quot;No-mind&quot; is just a paradigm-appropriate way for an Akashic to do it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7356-haldir2012","name":"haldir2012","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-02T07:55:37-08:00","id":1011081,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/789528-alternate-rules-for-practices-and-instruments\">Alternate Rules for Practices and Instruments</a> covers my thoughts on this.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-02T09:54:20-08:00","id":1011119,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>haldir2012</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1010956-what-is-fast-casting-also-what-should-a-rote-be-mechanically?p=1011075#post1011075\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Concerning rotes - they make the most sense to me as complex Effects that require major research to figure out, and which you can learn from a more experienced mage of your paradigm more quickly than you could figure it out on your own.<br /> <br /> Look at the Agama Re rite as an example. It's pretty complex, using three different spheres, and it allows you to travel the Underworld as a wraith. In the books, it doesn't seem that other mages with Entropy 4, Life 2, and Spirit 3 typically use/have that ability. So I'd call it a Rote; if you have the right sphere levels, you can learn it from another Euthanatos of the same paradigm and only then can you generate the Effect yourself. Otherwise, you'd need a good amount of research even after achieving the sphere requirements to replicate the Effect within your own paradigm - for example, a Son of Ether designing a Trans-Life Converter, or a Hermetic learning the right sigils to call upon Uriel, archangel of the threshold of death. I wouldn't allow a character who happens to have Entropy 4, Life 2, and Spirit 3 to randomly say without preparation, &quot;Okay, I go into the Underworld now.&quot;<br /> <br /> On the other end of the spectrum, simple effects don't really need rotes. The books list &quot;No-mind&quot; as a rote/common effect for the Akashics, whereby they empty their own mind of thoughts to better sense other minds. It's a one-dot effect for a single sphere, so I expect all characters with Mind 1 can come up with a way to do this on the fly. &quot;No-mind&quot; is just a paradigm-appropriate way for an Akashic to do it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Except that slapping the random appellation of &quot;Rote (et al.)&quot; on it means a -1 Diff. Which, apparently, is a big deal, though I've never seen it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-02T09:57:39-08:00","id":1011120,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ambrosia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1010956-what-is-fast-casting-also-what-should-a-rote-be-mechanically?p=1010991#post1010991\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> And the one that simply means &quot;Casting without a rote&quot;. Which essentially boils down to &quot;Making up a way to achieve an effect on the fly&quot;.<br /> The latter simply translates to the Mage, due to pressure and/or sudden need to react, works within their Paradigm and Belief system to quickly come up with a makeshift way of casting an effect.<br /> Essentially creating a new, unpracticed groundwork for what later can become a new Rote in their arsenal through practice and refinement.<br /> <br /> Both work, and I think both work well for the definition of Fast-Casting as a whole. Having to pull off a Rote quicker and will less refinement than the Rote usually requires, and making up something on the fly that can later become a Rote.</div> </div> </div> </div>How is your second one different, in almost any way, from pretty much any spontaneous casting (e.g. any non-Rote dynamic casting), in effect, from pretty much what every mage does pretty much every time they use their magic? Do you consistently apply a +1 Diff to all non-Rote casting and -1 to all Rote casting? With, I guess, a middle ground where there's non-Rote casting that takes extra time to have the + and - offset each other?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}